<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<p:notes xmlns:a="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main" xmlns:r="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships" xmlns:p="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/presentationml/2006/main"><p:cSld><p:spTree><p:nvGrpSpPr><p:cNvPr id="1" name=""/><p:cNvGrpSpPr/><p:nvPr/></p:nvGrpSpPr><p:grpSpPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="0" y="0"/><a:ext cx="0" cy="0"/><a:chOff x="0" y="0"/><a:chExt cx="0" cy="0"/></a:xfrm></p:grpSpPr><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="2" name="Slide Image Placeholder 1"/><p:cNvSpPr><a:spLocks noGrp="1" noRot="1" noChangeAspect="1"/></p:cNvSpPr><p:nvPr><p:ph type="sldImg"/></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr/></p:sp><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="3" name="Notes Placeholder 2"/><p:cNvSpPr><a:spLocks noGrp="1"/></p:cNvSpPr><p:nvPr><p:ph type="body" idx="1"/></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr/><p:txBody><a:bodyPr/><a:lstStyle/><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>Introduce baseline (</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0" err="1"/><a:t>notagen</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t> with no fine-tune -&gt; 3 columns)</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>ARROW UP and DOWN</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>VISUALIZATION WITH BAR CHART</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>To find out, I compared the fine-tuned NotaGen model against a baseline model that has a similar design but no pre-trained musical knowledge.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>I evaluated their output across three dimensions: melodies, rhythms, and the overall structure.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>We can see from the table that NotaGen produces results closer to the real data and sounds more melodic and smoother than the baseline output.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>Let’s hear the generated sample from each model.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>(play * 2)</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>This shows that the pre-trained music data was actually very helpful, so even though guzheng music sounds very different from Western music, we should still use the transfer learning method.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:endParaRPr></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:rPr><a:t>To wrap up …</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:rPr><a:t>---</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:endParaRPr></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>First, melodic quality — do the notes sound related to each other, and do the intervals between them feel smooth? </a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>NotaGen produced related notes and smooth melodies, while the baseline was essentially random with huge, unnatural leaps between notes.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>Second, rhythmic quality — is the timing of notes consistent or chaotic? </a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>The baseline had wildly erratic rhythms, while NotaGen's output was stable and natural.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>Third, structural quality — does the music repeat motifs and have recognizable patterns, the way real music does? </a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"/><a:t>The baseline never repeated anything — it was essentially noise. NotaGen's output had recurring motifs and compressible structure, much closer to real guzheng music.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:endParaRPr></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:endParaRPr></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:rPr><a:t>Explain why one sounds better than the other</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:rPr><a:t>Audio sample from each model, highlight 1-2 important metrics, (trained all on my laptop), audio as final result</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:rPr><a:t>A table + 2 audio sample</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:endParaRPr></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:endParaRPr></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:rPr><a:t>Put music on my phone</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:rPr><a:t>Highlight the rows that are important</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2400" dirty="0"><a:latin typeface="Bierstadt" panose="020B0004020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/></a:rPr><a:t>take-away</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" dirty="0"/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="4" name="Slide Number Placeholder 3"/><p:cNvSpPr><a:spLocks noGrp="1"/></p:cNvSpPr><p:nvPr><p:ph type="sldNum" sz="quarter" idx="5"/></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr/><p:txBody><a:bodyPr/><a:lstStyle/><a:p><a:fld id="{C4AF924F-90EE-1B49-BE73-09C71E579B62}" type="slidenum"><a:rPr lang="en-US" smtClean="0"/><a:t>7</a:t></a:fld><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US"/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp></p:spTree><p:extLst><p:ext uri="{BB962C8B-B14F-4D97-AF65-F5344CB8AC3E}"><p14:creationId xmlns:p14="http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/powerpoint/2010/main" val="4169391190"/></p:ext></p:extLst></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:notes>